# Android Studio will place build artifacts here
# Kotlin's incremental build state. Snake Classic ignores this and we did not;
# nothing of ours is tracked from it yet, which is luck rather than design.
android/.kotlin/

/android/app/debug
/android/app/profile
/android/app/release

# Widget Preview related
.widget_preview/

# ---- Pourfect: secrets & credentials ----
# Firebase client config (contains project keys; regenerate with flutterfire configure)
# Firebase config. These carry the project id, app id and Web API key.
#
# They are NOT cryptographic secrets — every one of these values ships inside
# the APK and can be read out of any downloaded build, which is why Firebase
# leans on Security Rules, App Check and API-key restrictions rather than on
# hiding them. They stay out of the repo anyway: it is the same convention
# Snake Classic uses, it keeps an unrestricted key from being scraped out of a
# public repo and burned on quota, and there is no upside to carrying them.
#
# Globs, not fixed paths — a second flavour or platform folder must not
# quietly reintroduce one.
**/google-services.json
**/GoogleService-Info.plist
**/google_service_client_secret.json
**/firebase_options.dart
**/.firebaserc
firebase.json

# Android signing
**/android/key.properties
*.jks
*.keystore

# Local env
#
# WARNING: .env is declared as a Flutter ASSET so the app can read it, which
# means it is bundled into the APK and trivially extractable. Gitignoring it
# is repo hygiene, NOT secrecy. Nothing that must stay private — a signing
# key, a service account, an API secret — may ever go in here. Public
# identifiers and endpoint URLs only.
*.env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
